{
  "title": "OpenProject \u2014 productivity (15,134\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: OpenProject\nDomain: productivity | Scale: large | Stars: 15,134\nProblem: Teams and organizations need a comprehensive, open-source web-based platform to manage projects, tasks, and collaboration. OpenProject provides a self-hostable alternative to proprietary project management tools with features spanning agile, planning, and reporting.\nTarget users: Software development teams, Project managers, Product managers, Scrum/agile teams, Organizations requiring self-hosted or on-premises project management, Enterprise teams needing compliance and data control\nCore features: Work package tracking and task management; Gantt chart / project planning and scheduling; Agile boards (Kanban/Scrum); Time tracking, cost reporting, and budgeting; Product roadmap and release planning; Bug and issue tracking; Wiki, forums, and meeting management; BIM/IFC and BCF integration for construction projects\nIntegrations: GitHub (pull request linking), xeokit (IFC/BIM 3D model viewing), Redocly (OpenAPI documentation)\nTech stack: Angular (TypeScript) + Ruby on Rails + PostgreSQL (inferred from Rails conventions) + Docker, Nix, Shell scripts\nKey libraries: Angular, @xeokit/xeokit-gltf-to-xkt, @redocly/cli, Sass/SCSS\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Hybrid rendering architecture: Rails handles server-side logic and API while Angular powers rich interactive frontend modules, a pragmatic approach for incrementally modernizing a large Rails app.\n- BIM/IFC support via xeokit indicates domain-specific extensibility built into the core product, targeting construction and engineering verticals beyond typical project management tools.\n- Multi-edition strategy (Community, Enterprise cloud, Enterprise on-premises) is reflected in the codebase structure, requiring clean separation of open-source core from proprietary add-ons.\n- Extensive use of Ruby with a TypeScript frontend suggests a long-lived codebase gradually adopting modern frontend tooling without a full rewrite.",
